{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, -2","reason":"For the two numbers u and v, u^3+v^3=(u+v)^3-3uv(u+v)=1-3=-2. This identity also holds for complex numbers.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1966_AMC_12_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148"],"id":"symmetric-cube-identity","label":"Express the cubes through the sum and product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-cube-identity":["math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 29/2","reason":"Set t=10^z. Then x+y=t, x²+y²=10t, and xy=(t²-10t)/2. Hence x³+y³=(x+y)^3-3xy(x+y)=-t³/2+15t². Thus a=-1/2, b=15, and a+b=29/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"derive-the-coefficient-identity","label":"Express the cubic sum in 10^z","nodeIds":["cnm_149","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},{"id":"evaluate-at-zero","label":"Use a feasible triple with z = 0","nodeIds":["cnm_223","cnm_029","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_148","cnm_074"],"id":"solution:evaluate-at-zero:e373ac6b7aff","label":"Choose a feasible value that gives a+b","methodDirectNodeIds":{"evaluate-at-zero":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_029"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_029","cnm_h043","cnm_148","cnm_074"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_148","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_090"],"id":"solution:derive-the-coefficient-identity:e373ac6b7aff","label":"Derive the full cubic identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"derive-the-coefficient-identity":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_149"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_149","cnm_h043","cnm_148","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_090"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: (a-1/a)(a²+1+1/a²)","reason":"Apply u³-v³=(u-v)(u²+uv+v²) with u=a and v=1/a. Since uv=1, the middle term in the second factor is +1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The original expression requires a≠0.","The original expression and its factored form require a ≠ 0."],"questionId":"1952_AMC_12_Q03","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_164","cnm_016"],"id":"difference-of-cubes","label":"Recognize a difference of cubes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"difference-of-cubes":["math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_164","cnm_016"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3159","reason":"The nine paired differences are 7, 37, 91, 169, 271, 397, 547, 721, and 919. Their sum is 3159.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"simplify-consecutive-cubes","label":"Simplify each pair","methodDirectNodeIds":{"simplify-consecutive-cubes":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_h204","cnm_021"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_h204","cnm_021"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 0","reason":"Writing s=r+1/r gives r^3+1/r^3=s(s^2-3)=0 because s^2=3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["No nonzero real r satisfies the hypothesis. The stated value applies when complex r is allowed."],"questionId":"1952_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_h097"],"id":"q26_reciprocal_cube_factorization","label":"Factor the sum of cubes and check the domain","methodDirectNodeIds":{"q26_reciprocal_cube_factorization":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","cnm_156","cnm_h097"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 0 whenever the premise is satisfied over complex numbers","reason":"The identity a³+a^(-3)=(a+a^(-1))[(a+a^(-1))²-3] forces 0. But no real nonzero a satisfies the premise, since (a+a^(-1))²=a²+2+a^(-2)≥4.","status":"ambiguous"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The domain of a is unstated. The premise has complex solutions but no real solutions; C is the formal algebraic answer."],"questionId":"1954_AMC_12_Q40","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_156","cnm_h097"],"id":"cube-factorization-with-domain-check","label":"Check the domain and factor the sum of cubes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-factorization-with-domain-check":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_h102","cnm_148","cnm_156","cnm_h097"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. R(z)=-z","reason":"Modulo the polynomial z²+z+1, we have z³≡1. Since 2021=3·673+2, z^2021+1≡z²+1≡-z. This already has degree below two.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"polynomial-reduction","label":"Reduce powers using the divisor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polynomial-reduction":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reduce-exponents-by-three","label":"Reduce powers three steps at a time","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reduce-exponents-by-three":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:sum-difference-cubes"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 1 real pair and 2 nonreal complex pairs","reason":"Set t = x + 1. Then y = t² and ty = 1, so t³ = 1. Its roots are 1 and (-1 ± i√3)/2. The first gives (x,y) = (0,1), while the other two give nonreal pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Imaginary pairs in the historical choices means nonreal complex pairs, not pairs of purely imaginary coordinates."],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q43","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-the-cubic","label":"Factor the cubic","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-the-cubic":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_223"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_223"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_h100"],"id":"substitute-the-repeated-expression","label":"Substitute the repeated expression","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-the-repeated-expression":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h097"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h097","cnm_148","cnm_h100"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, n = -1","reason":"Adding the equations gives (n + 1)(x + y + z) = 3, impossible when n = -1. For every other real n, x = y = z = 1/(n + 1) satisfies all three equations.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1973_AMC_12_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cyclic-elimination","label":"Eliminate cyclically","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cyclic-elimination":["cnm_089","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_089","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, one value: a=2","reason":"Subtracting gives (a+1)(x+1)=0. If a=-1, the common quadratic is x²-x+1=0, whose discriminant is -3. Otherwise x=-1, which forces a=2 and satisfies both equations.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitute-parameter-to-cubic","label":"Eliminate the parameter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-parameter-to-cubic":["math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, p=m³-3mn","reason":"If the original roots are r,s, then r+s=-m and rs=n. Thus -p=r³+s³=(r+s)³-3rs(r+s)=-m³+3mn, giving p=m³-3mn. Also q=n³.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cube-sum-symmetric-identity","label":"Use the symmetric cube-sum identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-sum-symmetric-identity":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_032","cnm_146"],"id":"symmetric-cube-sum","label":"Express the cube sum using the root sum and product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-cube-sum":["cnm_226","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_226","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","cnm_032","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 440","reason":"We have x²+y²=4²-2(-2)=20 and x³+y³=4³-3(-2)(4)=88. The expression equals (x²+y²)(x³+y³)/(xy)², so its value is 20·88/4=440. The denominator is nonzero because xy=-2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-symmetric-expression","label":"Factor the symmetric expression","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-symmetric-expression":["cnm_162","cnm_148","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_148","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_162"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (2)","reason":"Let t=log 2. Then log 5=1-t, log 20=1+t, log 8=3t, and log 0.25=-2t. The expression becomes (1-t)^3+(1+t)^3-6t^2=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-of-cubes-simplification","label":"Simplify the paired cubes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-of-cubes-simplification":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_147","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_147","cnm_148"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"paired-cubes","label":"Rewrite using log 2","methodDirectNodeIds":{"paired-cubes":["cnm_h044","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_147"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 4","reason":"Since 1+w+w^2 = 0, the two factors equal -2w and -2w^2. Their product is 4w^3 = 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1971_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_142"],"id":"use-the-cubic-root-identity","label":"Use the relation among the powers of w","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-the-cubic-root-identity":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_h097","cnm_142"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 1","reason":"Let the two cube roots be u and v. Then uv=-3 and u³+v³=10. For t=u+v, t³+9t-10=0, or (t-1)(t²+t+10)=0. The quadratic has negative discriminant, so t=1, which is not listed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-of-cubes-equation","label":"Equation for the sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-of-cubes-equation":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","cnm_223"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","cnm_223"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_076","cnm_147"],"id":"equation-for-sum","label":"Find an equation for the sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equation-for-sum":["cnm_080","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_146","math:completing-square-expression"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_080","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_146","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_076","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 21","reason":"Modulo x²+x+1, x³≡1 and x+1≡-x², so the polynomial reduces to 1+x²ⁿ+x⁴ⁿ. This is 3 when 3 divides n and is 0 otherwise. Of the choices, only 21 is divisible by 3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_143","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_032"],"id":"cycle-polynomial-remainders","label":"Reduce powers using the divisor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cycle-polynomial-remainders":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_143","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_032"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 16","reason":"Write the primes as t - 1 and t + 1. Their cube difference is 6t^2 + 2 = 31106, giving t^2 = 5184 and t = 72. The primes are 71 and 73. Integers 74 through 78 are composite, and 79 is prime, so the requested digit sum is 7 + 9 = 16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-cube-difference","label":"Factor the cube difference and complete the square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-cube-difference":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_221","cnm_p157"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_221","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1","reason":"Since (x+1)(x²-x+1)=x³+1 and (x-1)(x²+x+1)=x³-1, each inner fraction is 1 wherever defined. The product is therefore 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["For real x, the original expression excludes x=1 and x=-1.","For real x, the original expression excludes x=−1 and x=1. These exclusions remain after simplification."],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q40","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_143","cnm_156","cnm_157"],"id":"recognize-cube-identities","label":"Recognize the cube identities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"recognize-cube-identities":["cnm_158","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_158","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_143","cnm_156","cnm_157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 4","reason":"The roots have modulus 2 and arguments 30,90,150,210,270,330 degrees. Only square root of 3 plus i and square root of 3 minus i have positive real part; their product is 3 + 1 = 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1990_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"real-quadratic-factorization","label":"Factor into real quadratic factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"real-quadratic-factorization":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h097"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h097"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_146"],"id":"factor-into-quadratics","label":"Factor into simple quadratic equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-into-quadratics":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h102","cnm_h097","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2√21","reason":"A={2,-1+i√3,-1-i√3}, while the second polynomial factors as (z-8)(z²-8), giving B={8,2√2,-2√2}. For b∈B, squared distances are (b-2)² or (b+1)²+3. Their maximum is 84, attained between 8 and either nonreal point of A, so the greatest distance is 2√21.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-and-complete-the-square","label":"Find A algebraically","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-complete-the-square":["math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h102"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-and-compare-squared-distances","label":"Factor and compare distances","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-compare-squared-distances":["math:sum-difference-cubes","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_151","cnm_h098","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","math:complex-quadratic-solutions","cnm_151","cnm_h098","cnm_h173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 42","reason":"Let the ages be d,t,h. Substituting t = 2h - 3d into the cubic equation gives 6(h - d)²(h - 4d) = 0. The branch h = d makes t negative, so h = 4d and t = 5d. Relative primality gives d = 1, hence 1² + 5² + 4² = 42.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Ages are interpreted as positive integers, as required by the relative-primality condition."],"questionId":"1972_AMC_12_Q34","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_027","cnm_p158","cnm_040"],"id":"matched-cube-factors","label":"Method 2: Match factors from the two equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"matched-cube-factors":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_154"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_154","cnm_027","cnm_p158","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. a=3x or a=-2x","reason":"Since x=a-b≠0, cancellation gives a²+ab+b²=19x². Substituting b=a-x yields a²-ax-6x²=0, or (a-3x)(a+2x)=0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_040","cnm_148","cnm_145"],"id":"cube-factorization","label":"Factor the difference of cubes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-factorization":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_224","cnm_040","cnm_148","cnm_145"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 970","reason":"Let α=√3+√2 and β=√3-√2. Expansion gives α⁶+β⁶=970, while 0<β<1 implies 0<β⁶<1. Thus 969<α⁶<970, and the requested integer is 970.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_173","cnm_148","cnm_096"],"id":"conjugate-power","label":"Pair the expression with its conjugate","methodDirectNodeIds":{"conjugate-power":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_147","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_147","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_173","cnm_148","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 7/2","reason":"Let A = 2ˣ - 4 and B = 4ˣ - 2. Since A³ + B³ - (A + B)³ = -3AB(A + B), one factor must vanish. These give x = 2, x = 1/2, or, with t = 2ˣ > 0, t² + t - 6 = 0 and hence x = 1. Their sum is 7/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1991_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cube-factorization","label":"Factor the cubic identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-factorization":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","cnm_151","cnm_220","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","cnm_151","cnm_220","cnm_224"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h042","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_148","cnm_220"],"id":"cube-identity-and-exponential-substitution","label":"Use the cube-of-a-sum structure","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-identity-and-exponential-substitution":["cnm_h049","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_224","math:quadratic-exponential-substitution"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h049","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_224","math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_h042","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_148","cnm_220"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3","reason":"Cancel a-b and set r=a/b. The equation becomes 70r²-149r+70=(10r-7)(7r-10)=0. Since r>1, r=10/7. Coprimality therefore forces a=10 and b=7, giving a-b=3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_10A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_158","cnm_p158","cnm_p168"],"id":"factor-quadratic","label":"Factor the quadratic relation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-quadratic":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_224","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_158","cnm_p158","cnm_p168"]},{"contextNodeIds":["math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_p158","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"solution:coprime-divisibility:1d19a8eaf1aa","label":"Use the coprimality of the difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coprime-divisibility":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:prime-factorization","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_p158","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, x^2 = 80","reason":"Let u = ∛(x+9) and v = ∛(x-9). Then u-v = 3 and 18 = u^3-v^3 = 27+9uv, so uv = -1. Cubing gives x^2-81 = -1, hence x^2 = 80. The candidates x = ±4√5 satisfy the original equation.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1963_AMC_12_Q40","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_076","cnm_143","cnm_147","cnm_074","cnm_146"],"id":"cube-root-product","label":"Find the product of the cube roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-root-product":["math:radical-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["math:radical-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_076","cnm_143","cnm_147","cnm_074","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (35)","reason":"The equation factors as (m+n-33)Q=0, where Q=m²+n²-mn+33m+33n+1089=[(m-n)²+(m+33)²+(n+33)²]/2. Thus Q=0 only at (-33,-33). On m+n=33, the condition mn≥0 gives the 34 pairs with m=0,1,...,33. Total: 35.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1999_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_014","cnm_081","cnm_146"],"id":"factor-and-use-nonnegative-squares","label":"Factor and use nonnegative squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-use-nonnegative-squares":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_148","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_014","cnm_081","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 962","reason":"Let F(k)=k²+k+1. Then a_k=F(k+1)/((k−1)F(k)), so the product is 2F(100)/(F(4)·98!)=962/98!. A denominator of 97! would require 962/98 to be integral, which it is not; smaller factorials are therefore impossible.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","cnm_p153","cnm_160"],"id":"base-expansion-telescoping","label":"Convert the repeating blocks and telescope","methodDirectNodeIds":{"base-expansion-telescoping":["cnm_024","math:infinite-geometric-series","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_024","math:infinite-geometric-series","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:telescoping","math:factorial-notation","cnm_p153","cnm_160"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: -3/2","reason":"For x≠±1, the fraction equals (x^2-x+1)/(x-1). Its limit as x approaches -1 is 3/(-2)=-3/2, which is the required extension value.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1952_AMC_12_Q36","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_h027"],"id":"1952_AMC_12_Q36_m1","label":"Cancel the factor causing the missing value","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1952_AMC_12_Q36_m1":["math:elementary-function-limits","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","cnm_158","cnm_029"]},"nodeIds":["math:elementary-function-limits","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","cnm_158","cnm_029","cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_h027"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":10,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, (3+√5)/2","reason":"Let R>r>0 be the bottom and top base radii and s the insphere radius. The axial section is an isosceles trapezoid circumscribed about a circle of radius s, with height2s. Equal tangent segments from its four corners show each sloping leg has length R+r. Its horizontal offset is R-r, so Pythagoras gives (R+r)^2=(R-r)^2+4s^2 and s^2=Rr. Extend to the full cone: similarity gives the small/full cone radius ratio r/R and corresponding height ratio; subtract their cone volumes to obtain V_frustum=(pi*(2s)/3)*(R^2+Rr+r^2). The exact sphere formula gives V_sphere=4*pi*s^3/3. The stated volume comparison reduces to R^2+Rr+r^2=4s^2=4Rr, so k=R/r satisfies k^2-3k+1=0. The quadratic root above 1 is (3+sqrt(5))/2, E. Both figures and the text supply an insphere tangent to both bases and the cone side; no diagram scale is used.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h106","math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_262","math:sum-difference-cubes"],"id":"solution:axial-tangents-and-cone-difference:997f986fe728","label":"Axial section and cone volumes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"axial-tangents-and-cone-difference":["math:sphere-volume","cnm_p209","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["math:sphere-volume","cnm_p209","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_222","cnm_h106","math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_262","math:sum-difference-cubes"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":12,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, (3+√5)/2","reason":"Let R>r>0 be the bottom and top base radii and s the insphere radius. The axial section is an isosceles trapezoid circumscribed about a circle of radius s, with height2s. Equal tangent segments from its four corners show each sloping leg has length R+r. Its horizontal offset is R-r, so Pythagoras gives (R+r)^2=(R-r)^2+4s^2 and s^2=Rr. Extend to the full cone: similarity gives the small/full cone radius ratio r/R and corresponding height ratio; subtract their cone volumes to obtain V_frustum=(pi*(2s)/3)*(R^2+Rr+r^2). The exact sphere formula gives V_sphere=4*pi*s^3/3. The stated volume comparison reduces to R^2+Rr+r^2=4s^2=4Rr, so k=R/r satisfies k^2-3k+1=0. The quadratic root above 1 is (3+sqrt(5))/2, E. Both figures and the text supply an insphere tangent to both bases and the cone side; no diagram scale is used.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_10B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h106","cnm_p162","cnm_p192","cnm_262","cnm_271","math:sum-difference-cubes"],"id":"solution:axial-tangents-and-cone-difference:456d1d83e4a9","label":"Use an axial section and subtract cone volumes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"axial-tangents-and-cone-difference":["math:sphere-volume","math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_272","cnm_p209","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["math:sphere-volume","math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_272","cnm_p209","cnm_222","cnm_h106","cnm_p162","cnm_p192","cnm_262","cnm_271","math:sum-difference-cubes"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:sum-difference-cubes","status":"mapped","total":31}
